Rebased onto the latest `main` and resolved the conflicts.

@ch-wan @hnyls2002 — bumping this one. Request preprocessing on the tokenizer-manager path runs inline on the event loop, so a burst of large requests starves it: `/health` and the liveness path stop being served while the engine itself is perfectly fine. Under an orchestrator with a liveness probe, that turns a transient load spike into a restart of a healthy process. The PR moves the blocking part off the loop and adds CPU-only regression tests covering HTTP liveness during preprocessing.

**On the red checks:** all `pr-gate` / `pr-test-*-finish`, failing for a missing `run-ci` label rather than any real test failure — I'm not in `.github/CI_PERMISSIONS.json` so I can't trigger the lanes myself. Could a maintainer add `run-ci`? The three new tests are CPU-only and should be quick.

@yongqinwang-cmd thank you — that's a much better validation than anything I could produce on my own, and the numbers line up exactly with what this patch is meant to fix. Independent reproduction on a different model and a different deployment, with a measured before/after (4 readiness timeouts in the first minute → zero over 10+ minutes at 448 concurrent clients with ~64k-token inputs), is the strongest evidence this PR has.

Worth stating plainly why the failure mode is worse than it looks: the health endpoint going quiet does not mean the engine is unhealthy. Inference keeps making progress the whole time — it is only the event loop that cannot get a turn to answer the probe. So an orchestrator with a liveness probe restarts a server that was working, drops every in-flight request on it, and the restart lands the load on the remaining replicas, which then starve the same way. A transient burst turns into a rolling outage, and nothing in the logs points at preprocessing.

@ch-wan @hnyls2002 @merrymercy — given a second party is now carrying this as a local patch in production, could we get this looked at? It is confined to the tokenizer-manager preprocessing path and ships three CPU-only regression tests.

The blocker is still purely mechanical: the red checks are `pr-gate` / `pr-test-*-finish` refusing to start because the PR has no `run-ci` label, so no test has ever actually run on it. I am not in `.github/CI_PERMISSIONS.json`, so `/tag-run-ci-label` is a no-op when I post it. Adding the label is all that is needed to get real signal here.

@hnyls2002 @merrymercy I pushed `e0a728540` with a smaller scope informed by the shared-tokenizer review on #39716: regular HF fast tokenization now uses a startup-cloned native backend, passed explicitly to its worker. Slow/custom tokenizers retain their existing path. The OpenAI conversion hunk already merged in #39771 is removed.

The 6 focused regressions pass (the unmodified base fails the 2 starvation cases); the related CPU suite passes 388 tests + 376 subtests. A synthetic probe with pinned tokenizers 0.23.2 also confirms that loop-side padding no longer waits behind this worker's backend. GPU short-prompt TTFT at low/high concurrency remains unvalidated.

Could you review this isolation boundary and authorize `run-ci` for the current head? The [current gate](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/41331127133/job/124921265674) stops at the missing label. I have also asked the authors of #39716/#44502 to consolidate into one merge path; I am happy to move this focused fix and its tests into #39716 with attribution if that is preferable.

@hnyls2002 @merrymercy could you help choose the merge path for the remaining regular fast-tokenizer fix and authorize CI on `e0a728540bb6`?

#39771 already covers OpenAI conversion; this PR is limited to regular HF fast tokenization with a separate worker-owned backend. The recorded CPU suite passes 388 tests plus 376 subtests, including starvation regressions that fail on the unmodified base. Slow/custom tokenizers keep their synchronous behavior. GPU serving/short-prompt TTFT validation remains outstanding.

#39716 and #44502 are still open and overlap this work. Would you prefer this three-file fix as a prerequisite, or fold its implementation and regressions into one of those PRs with attribution preserved? I would like to avoid parallel merge paths. The current upstream tests are blocked on `run-ci`; I am not treating the local results as CI approval.

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Offloading tokenization to a separate thread via `asyncio.to_thread` is a great improvement for event loop responsiveness. However, if the tokenizer is a slow tokenizer (i.e., `not getattr(self.tokenizer, "is_fast", False)`), it is implemented in pure Python and is generally not thread-safe. Running it concurrently in multiple threads can lead to race conditions, corrupted tokenization outputs, or crashes.

Furthermore, since pure Python code does not release the GIL, running multiple slow tokenizations concurrently in separate threads does not provide any CPU parallelization benefits anyway. 

To ensure thread safety for slow tokenizers while keeping the asyncio event loop responsive, we should serialize calls to slow tokenizers using a lock (e.g., a `threading.Lock` initialized on the manager).

```python
            if not hasattr(self, "_tokenizer_lock"):
                self._tokenizer_lock = threading.Lock()

            def tokenize_sync():
                is_fast = getattr(self.tokenizer, "is_fast", False)
                if not is_cross_encoder and not is_fast:
                    with self._tokenizer_lock:
                        return [self.tokenizer.encode(t) for t in tokenizer_input], None

                if not is_fast:
                    with self._tokenizer_lock:
                        encoded = self.tokenizer(tokenizer_input, **tokenizer_kwargs)
                else:
                    encoded = self.tokenizer(tokenizer_input, **tokenizer_kwargs)

                return (
                    encoded["input_ids"],
                    encoded.get("token_type_ids") if is_cross_encoder else None,
                )

            input_ids, token_type_ids = await asyncio.to_thread(tokenize_sync)
```

